the coronavirus pandemic is causing a  mental health crisis  among the front line health care workers  one study found that 57 percent  57 percent have experienced acute stress  and 48 percent  exhibited depressive symptoms now the  doctors and the nurses tell us they've  been working relentlessly for months  and now they are dealing with new  challenges and fears as a number of  hospitalizations  hits a record high things are pretty  tough right now for us  our med surg beds are pretty much at  capacity  and then we have about 10 of our staff  that are out quarantined  so we're facing staff shortages it's  kind of an exercise in  system stress tolerance how much can we  take until the wheels fall off are you  seeing your hospital where you're  working being  overwhelmed with patients we're  certainly getting a major  influx we're having to manufacture new  places to put people we're looking at a  situation where icus are going to be  full  even if we can create physical beds we  just won't have the staff  that we need to be able to care for  people the way they need to be cared for  what is it like emotionally at this  point  healthcare workers are carrying an  enormous emotional burden and toll  from the last several months uh just the  level of  fatigue and accumulated trauma our  health care providers have faced  already we're at a point where people  should really be looking to replenish  themselves and instead of replenishing  themselves  they're climbing the highest part of the  mountain i think exhaustion is the  biggest  the nurses here are working a minimum of  four 12-hour shifts a week  and then on top of it having all the  schools be out so you're basically  working a 12-hour shift and  you're going home at night and trying to  homeschool your your children  and you're doing that day after day  after day  i think a lot of us now are are in kind  of for the lack of a word siege  mentality  where emotional considerations just kind  of get pushed aside for a while  it is fatigue beyond fatigue at the  point where we're  most worn down we actually have to do  something that is  exponentially probably going to be more  work than what we've done  and dealt with before and we can't stay  home and work from home  we show up every day and we do the best  we can but you still have to deal with  that mental toll it does take on you  that horrifying fear in the back of your  mind that  at some point people are just going to  die  because there's no place for them that  gives me pause  i do believe that there is this  reckoning with how vulnerable we all  are to the virus to the possibility of  being  overwhelmed by it and death i think is  something that i grapple with as a  doctor but it never became so  tangible until now what do you want  people to know  about how health care workers are  feeling going into  another surge no matter what we are here  and in the same way that we'll always be  here for you we also want  people to really do all the things we  know we can do to stop transmission  at the end of the day we're putting one  foot in front of the other and showing  up  every morning and that is 99 percent of  the battle  wow our senior medical correspondent dr  tara narula is following the effect  of the pandemic on the front line  workers and joins us now tara i remember  when you did a story like this  back in march it hadn't even been a  month i remember thinking god i never  thought of that before  how these people must be feeling who are  literally risking their lives to save  ours  so what is at stake for them right now  what is the biggest challenge for them  well gail you heard the fatigue is  certainly a challenge but it really is  this sense of being in a marathon  and it's just a relentless pace that  doesn't let up for all of these months  the concern that they're just going to  as dr nielsen said drown  under the weight of all of these  patients how are they going to take care  of them  but one of the other biggest challenges  is feeling like people still  aren't taking this seriously walking out  of the hospital seeing people not  wearing masks  in lines packed together waiting to get  into a bar and that's why many of them  have turned to  television or social media to tell those  personal stories thinking maybe that  will make an  impact this is what we're witnessing  inside the hospital  dr dillon in the piece telling us one of  the darkest moments for him  was telling two teenagers that their  father died in his icu  and then three days later that their  mother died all of this over the phone  without these kids being able to say  goodbye to their parents  to see them so these are the stories  that we hope will make an impact on  people so they take these mitigation  strategies seriously they must go nuts  when they walk around and see people  being so careless and reckless  i heard one doctor say it's like running  a marathon what you just said  and then being asked to do the ironman  right after that there's just no let up  do they have any silver linings do you  see any silver linings for them  well certainly they talked about the  hope associated with a vaccine  and also hearing these survivor stories  and seeing survivors that gives them the  strength to keep going  they also talked about the camaraderie  being a silver lining that they really  bonded being in the trenches together  but as dr nielsen said to us there is a  light at the end of the tunnel  but it's a very long tunnel still gosh  tara thank you very much i think these  stories are so important to remind  people again  wear your mask
 

